 2. Stock investors keep the AI faith 
 
 By Emily Peck 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Stocks are the new bonds in AI — Anthropic and Meta have turned to equity investors in looking for billions of dollars.

 Why it matters: After plowing billions into AI companies, bond investors have lately grown skeptical of funding open-ended asks for money. 

 Stock market investors, however, are all about throwing money at hopes and dreams even when the future payout may be hard to see. The big picture: The AI buildout requires a steady stream of investments and investors to stick with ambitious spending plans. 

 To get that cash, companies are pulling every available lever: stocks, private loans and project financing, which has soared this year , as well as bonds. Catch up quick: Equity-based fundraising announced Monday reached nearly $200 billion in a single day. 

 State of play: The $100 billion Anthropic wants to raise with its IPO, as well as $95 billion in Meta fundraising, all seemed to rekindle investors' zest for AI. The tech-heavy Nasdaq closed up for the day. And some AI stocks popped.

 "Many of the AI jitters are melting away," said Steve Sosnick, chief strategist at Interactive Brokers. Follow the money: That enthusiasm for all things AI in the wake of Anthropic should help sales of shares to the kinds of investors who "either believe the company is a big winner in a brave new world, or they don't," in the words of a Financial Times Lex column .

 Zoom in: In contrast, the open-ended promise of the AI buildout counts against these companies when it comes to the credit markets.

